/target

# Secondary CARGO_TARGET_DIRs. Concurrent agents share the primary `target/`
# (cargo's own lock serialises them) and use a separate one for the MSRV
# toolchain so a 1.85 build does not evict the stable artifacts.
/target-msrv

# Worktrees the agent harness creates for parallel work. They contain full
# checkouts, so an unguarded `git add -A` from this directory would commit a
# copy of the repository into itself.
/.claude/worktrees/

# Office lock files. Word, Excel and PowerPoint write `~$<name>` beside a file while it
# is open, and it carries the user name of whoever holds it -- the very thing the fixture
# metadata is scrubbed of, appearing in tests/fixtures/ exactly while that scrubbing is
# being done. The tarball allowlist globs `tests/fixtures/*` on purpose, so without this
# `cargo package` picks one up: verified with `cargo package --list`.
~$*

# The [MS-OFFCRYPTO] specification PDF, which this crate is written against and cites
# throughout. Microsoft publishes it under the Open Specification Promise -- a patent
# promise that grants no redistribution right -- so the document is read here and never
# committed. 3.8 MB of someone else's copyright is also two thirds of what the whole
# repository weighs.
#
# This lived in .git/info/exclude, which is per-clone and does not travel: a fresh clone
# had no such rule, so the first `git add -A` beside a downloaded copy would have committed
# it. Ignore rules that protect the repository belong in the repository.
MS-OFFCRYPTO.pdf
